  :adr/body "## The report

「終わったけれど、戻ってきません」-- the sign-in completed in the browser and the
CLI kept waiting.

## Two bugs, both mine, both in the half I could not finish

The flow I landed yesterday was verified as far as starting it: the URL came
back, the browser opened, the poll ran. I said at the time that I could not
complete the ceremony myself and that the completed login was unverified. Both
faults are on the other side of that line.

**The claim was never admitted.** `/api/auth/itonami/handoff` computes
`{:origin-trusted? (same-origin? exchange config)}`, and `same-origin?`
compares the request's `Origin` header against the server's own origin. The CLI
sent none, so the claim could never be admitted and the endpoint answered
`{:ready? false}` forever -- which is the same answer it gives a wrong guess,
an early poll, a replay and an expiry, on purpose. Nothing in the response
could tell the poller it was asking wrongly.

Measured: no Origin → 403, wrong Origin → 403, `http://localhost:1338` → 200.
The CLI now computes that value the way the server does (`:public-origin`, else
host:port) rather than reusing `base-url`, which on this install is a different
string (`127.0.0.1`) for the same server.

**The token was never in the body.** On success the route sends
`{:ready? :provider :linked?}` and puts the session in a `Set-Cookie` header --
the way a browser is given one. The client read `(:token body)`, which does not
exist. So even with the Origin right, the poll would have seen `:ready? true`
and stored nothing.

`request!` now returns `:set-cookie`, and `text/cookie-value` parses it. That
parser is in `itonami-text` rather than the launcher so it can be tested: a
cookie parser that quietly returns nothing is a sign-in that quietly never
finishes. The control -- matching a name that merely ENDS with the wanted one
-- fails the check by name.

## What this says about the first attempt

The flow was landed with its completion untested and a note saying so. The note
was true and did not help: two bugs sat exactly there, and one of them
(`:token` in the body) is visible in the route's own source, three lines from
the `Set-Cookie` it sends instead.

Reading the endpoint I was calling would have caught it. `I cannot run this
end to end` is a reason to read the other side more carefully, not less.

## WalletConnect is not a sign-in on this server, and cannot be made one here

The ask was for wallet access as an alternative. Measured:

- `POST /api/wallet/connect/start` calls `require-human-session!` before
  anything else, then `identity/session-principal-id session` and refuses
  without one.
- `finish-connection!` checks the SIWE transaction against `(:user-id session)`
  and `(:organization-id session)`.

So SIWE here proves *this wallet belongs to the already-authenticated user*. It
is an attachment, not an authentication -- there is no route that takes a
signature from someone with no session and mints one.

Adding that is a new authentication path into the identity system, and it needs
answers this cannot invent: whether a wallet may CREATE an account or only sign
in to one that already linked it; which User an unknown address maps to; what
happens when an address is linked to two Users; whether a wallet session is
`:kind :passkey` or something weaker, given `require-human-session!` is the
money surface. Those are the owner's decisions, and getting them wrong is
account takeover.

So it is not built here. What is true today: sign in with the Passkey once, and
the wallet can be attached after that through the routes that already exist.
"}]
